ap´&0ìªSÀ[þ‚âä;ÖB^·ŸÀð)ì·ï½{"rendered":{"html":"<p>GCSL programs compute values. A block can return one value or construct a\nrecord of named fields, so you can use configuration data inside ordinary\nexpressions without a separate data format.</p>\n<h2 id=\"language-values-and-bindings--blocks-and-records\">Blocks and records <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#language-values-and-bindings--blocks-and-records\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Newlines or semicolons separate items inside a file or <code>{ ... }</code> block.\nA block without fields returns its final expression, or an explicit\n<code>= expression</code> result. A block with fields returns their record instead;\nit cannot also have an explicit result. The empty record <code>{}</code> represents\nan operation with no useful result.</p>\n<p>A field is a public part of the resulting record. A <code>let</code> binding is local\nto its scope. This block keeps the intermediate name private:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let release = \"0.1.0\"\nname = \"gcsl\"\narchive = \"{name}-{release}.tar.gz\"\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Top-level fields and named functions can refer to declarations written\nlater. Inner blocks evaluate their ordinary fields in order. Private\n<code>let</code> bindings become available after their declaration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"language-values-and-bindings--updating-a-binding\">Updating a binding <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#language-values-and-bindings--updating-a-binding\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>:=</code> replaces the value associated with a local name. For an ordinary\n<code>let</code>, this is restricted to the block that declares it. Use <code>let mut</code>\nwhen an inner block of the same function needs to update the variable:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let mut total = 0\nfor cost in [3, 8, 5] {\n    total := total + cost\n}\ntotal\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The variable keeps its declared or inferred type. Values themselves do not\nchange: replacing a record field or list element constructs an updated\nvalue for the binding. Other bindings and previously created closures\nkeep the values they already held.</p>\n<p>A closure captures values when it is created. It cannot update a mutable\nvariable belonging to its enclosing function. Pass in a value and return\nan updated value when you need that communication.</p>\n<h2 id=\"language-values-and-bindings--names-and-arithmetic\">Names and arithmetic <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#language-values-and-bindings--names-and-arithmetic\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>Names can contain hyphens. Write spaces around subtraction so that\n<code>left - right</code> is an operation rather than a name. Integer arithmetic uses\nsigned 64-bit values with checked overflow. Floating-point values use\n64-bit precision. <code>/</code> produces a floating-point result; <code>//</code> and <code>%</code> are\ninteger floor division and modulo.</p>\n<p>Integer literals adapt to an expected numeric type. A bound <code>Int</code> does\nnot silently become a <code>Float</code>; use <code>float(value)</code> when that conversion is\nintended. Conditions require <code>Bool</code>, not a nonzero integer or a nonempty\nstring. See <code>gcsl(5)</code> for syntax and operator rules.</p>\n<h2 id=\"language-values-and-bindings--durations-and-sizes\">Durations and sizes <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#language-values-and-bindings--durations-and-sizes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>Duration</code> and <code>Size</code> hold amounts with units. Write a duration as a\nnumber followed by units from largest to smallest: <code>30s</code>, <code>500ms</code>,\n<code>1h30m</code>, <code>2d</code>. The units are <code>d</code>, <code>h</code>, <code>m</code>, <code>s</code>, <code>ms</code>, <code>us</code> and <code>ns</code>, and a\nday is exactly 24 hours. Write a size with one unit: <code>512B</code>, the decimal\n<code>KB</code> to <code>PB</code>, or the binary <code>KiB</code> to <code>PiB</code>, as in <code>2GiB</code>. The last part of\na literal can have a fraction if the result is a whole number of\nnanoseconds or bytes, so <code>1.5s</code> and <code>0.5KiB</code> are allowed but <code>1.5ns</code> is\nnot. Units are case-sensitive.</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let timeout = 1m30s\nretry_after = timeout // 3\nbudget = 2 * timeout\nmemory = 2GiB + 512MiB\nlong = timeout &gt; 1m\nmessage = \"giving up after {timeout}\"\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Add or subtract two values of the same kind, multiply one by an <code>Int</code>,\nand use <code>//</code> to divide by an <code>Int</code> or by another value of the same kind.\nDividing by the same kind gives an <code>Int</code>. <code>%</code> gives the remainder of one\nvalue by another of the same kind. Values of the same kind can be\ncompared. Durations and sizes never mix with each other, with <code>Int</code> or\nwith <code>Float</code>, and an integer is never a duration, so write <code>0s</code> rather\nthan <code>0</code>. <code>/</code> is not available, because the result would not be a whole\nnumber of the smallest unit.</p>\n<p>A value is shown as its simplest literal: <code>retry_after</code> above is <code>30s</code>,\n<code>budget</code> is <code>3m</code>, and <code>memory</code> is <code>2560MiB</code>.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"language-values-and-bindings--blocks-and-records","title":"Blocks and records","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"language-values-and-bindings--updating-a-binding","title":"Updating a binding","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"language-values-and-bindings--names-and-arithmetic","title":"Names and arithmetic","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"language-values-and-bindings--durations-and-sizes","title":"Durations and sizes","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[],"anchors":[]},"refs":null}